D’Hondt et al. Sports Medicine - Open (2026) 12:146 Sports Medicine - Open | https://doi.org/10.1186/s40798-026-01082-1 | SYSTEMATIC REVIEW Open Access | The Relationship Between Inter‑limb | Asymmetry and Sprinting Performance in Sprint | Athletes: A Systematic Review | Joachim D’Hondt1* , Laurent Chapelle1 , Jarno Van Handenhoven1, Jasper Stalmans1 and | Dirk Aerenhouts1 | Abstract | Background Inter‑limb asymmetries are commonly observed in athletes, yet their relationship with sprinting perfor‑ | mance remains poorly understood. While most existing research has focused on unilateral sports, a comprehensive | systematic review examining the relationship between lower limb asymmetry and sprinting performance in sprint | athletes is still lacking. | Objective This systematic review aimed to examine the association between lower inter-limb asymmetry and sprint‑ | ing performance in sprint athletes. | Methods A systematic search was conducted in PubMed, Scopus, SPORTDiscus and Web of Science to identify | studies investigating lower inter-limb asymmetry and sprinting performance in healthy, injury-free sprint athletes. | The review was conducted in accordance with PRISMA 2020 guidelines (PROSPERO: CRD420251140520). Study | quality was assessed using the Downs and Black Quality Index Tool, whilst the evidence certainty was evaluated | with the GRADE framework. | Results Of 4,811 articles screened, 10 studies met the inclusion criteria. In general, limited associations were | observed between functional, morphological, kinetic and kinematic asymmetry and sprinting performance. However, | task- and metric-specific associations were observed in individual studies. | Conclusions Given the limited number of eligible studies and the substantial heterogeneity in study designs, | tasks, and asymmetry metrics, drawing definitive conclusions remains challenging. The evidence synthesized in this | review is mixed, and therefore does not allow for a straightforward assertion that inter-limb asymmetry is associated | with reduced sprinting performance. To enhance comparability and clarify the potential role of inter-limb asymmetry | in sprinting, future research should implement standardized, task-specific, metric-specific, and muscle-specific assess‑ | ment approaches. | Key Points | • The inconsistent evidence on the relationship between inter‑limb asymmetry and sprinting performance | in sprint athletes indicates that reducing asymmetry does not necessarily need to be a central focus of training. | *Correspondence: | Joachim D’Hondt | joachim.dhondt@vub.be | Full list of author information is available at the end of the article | © The Author(s) 2026. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which | permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the | original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or | other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line

